<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?><rss xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:itunes="http://www.itunes.com/dtds/podcast-1.0.dtd" version="2.0"><channel><title>Popular posts on daily.dev</title><link>https://app.daily.dev/posts</link><atom:link href="http://rsshub.rssforever.com/daily/popular" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"></atom:link><description>daily.dev is the easiest way to stay updated on the latest programming news. Get the best content from the top tech publications on any topic you want. - Powered by RSSHub</description><generator>RSSHub</generator><webMaster>contact@rsshub.app (RSSHub)</webMaster><language>en-us</language><lastBuildDate>Tue, 01 Sep 2026 03:04:06 GMT</lastBuildDate><ttl>5</ttl><item><title>Tether Brings the Apple Continuity Experience to Linux</title><description>&lt;img src=&quot;https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto/v1/posts/3c7793c7fc17ec6723b1e80cfe2120e7?_a=AQAEuop&quot; referrerpolicy=&quot;no-referrer&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;Zack Bartel built Tether, an open source MIT-licensed project that brings Apple Continuity-style features to Linux without needing a Mac. It handles clipboard sync, file transfer, OTP autofill, iMessage, SMS, notifications, and contact sync between an iPhone and a Linux machine, using a background daemon (tetherd), a GTK4 app, and a SwiftUI iOS companion app that discovers the daemon via Bonjour. Bluetooth groundwork came from ancs4linux and BlueFerry, but Zack did a clean-room C++ rewrite to keep the license MIT. Pairing security is handled with mTLS, and the codebase gets regular AI-assisted security sweeps. Tether is installable via AUR on Arch Linux or .deb/.rpm packages on GitHub, requires iOS 26.1+ on the iPhone side, and needs Firefox and Thunderbird extensions for OTP autofill to work fully.</description><link>https://daily.dev/posts/tether-brings-the-apple-continuity-experience-to-linux-2af00aqvi</link><guid isPermaLink="false">https://api.daily.dev/r/2Af00AQVi</guid><pubDate>Mon, 31 Aug 2026 13:02:33 GMT</pubDate><itunes:image href="https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto/v1/posts/3c7793c7fc17ec6723b1e80cfe2120e7?_a=AQAEuop"></itunes:image><category>open-source</category><category>linux</category><category>mac</category></item><item><title></title><description>&lt;img src=&quot;https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/s--VDukGCjf--/f_auto/v1722860399/public/Placeholder%2002&quot; referrerpolicy=&quot;no-referrer&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;</description><link>https://daily.dev/posts/woqztduc5</link><guid isPermaLink="false">https://api.daily.dev/r/WOQZTduc5</guid><pubDate>Mon, 31 Aug 2026 09:57:25 GMT</pubDate><author>Miodrag Todorovic</author><itunes:image href="https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/s--VDukGCjf--/f_auto/v1722860399/public/Placeholder%2002"></itunes:image><category>ai-agents</category><category>typescript</category></item><item><title></title><description>&lt;img src=&quot;https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/s--2-1xRawN--/f_auto/v1722860399/public/Placeholder%2011&quot; referrerpolicy=&quot;no-referrer&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;</description><link>https://daily.dev/posts/7ygt7o4rv</link><guid isPermaLink="false">https://api.daily.dev/r/7yGt7o4RV</guid><pubDate>Sun, 30 Aug 2026 16:59:46 GMT</pubDate><author>Ragunathan Maniraj</author><itunes:image href="https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/s--2-1xRawN--/f_auto/v1722860399/public/Placeholder%2011"></itunes:image><category>google</category></item><item><title>A Raspberry Pi taught me what actually matters in self-hosting, and it&#39;s not what the homelab subreddits obsess over</title><description>&lt;img src=&quot;https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto/v1/posts/0de09f89d7b04cba55ae4c38fbb1c146?_a=AQAEuop&quot; referrerpolicy=&quot;no-referrer&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;A personal account of building a low-cost homelab using a Raspberry Pi Zero 2 W and repurposed hardware, arguing that self-hosting doesn&#39;t require expensive server racks or clusters as seen on homelab subreddits. The author ran Jellyfin, Home Assistant, Nextcloud, and eventually lightweight Docker (via DietPi) on a budget SBC, later scaling gradually by adding SSDs, a repurposed HDD, and memory upgrades to an old laptop, emphasizing testing, patience, and backups over premature hardware investment.</description><link>https://daily.dev/posts/a-raspberry-pi-taught-me-what-actually-matters-in-self-hosting-and-it-s-not-what-the-homelab-subred-hdhmw8k9o</link><guid isPermaLink="false">https://api.daily.dev/r/HDhmw8k9o</guid><pubDate>Sun, 30 Aug 2026 15:01:52 GMT</pubDate><itunes:image href="https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto/v1/posts/0de09f89d7b04cba55ae4c38fbb1c146?_a=AQAEuop"></itunes:image><category>docker</category><category>self-hosting</category><category>raspberry-pi</category><category>homelab</category></item><item><title>Microservices Are Not a Sign You’re a Senior Engineer</title><description>&lt;img src=&quot;https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto/v1/posts/7cc99c8e6cd480acc14c1304004cfa46?_a=AQAEuop&quot; referrerpolicy=&quot;no-referrer&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;Complexity like microservices, Kafka, and Kubernetes is often mistaken for a marker of seniority, but true engineering skill lies in knowing when such complexity is actually needed. A well-designed modular monolith can offer clear boundaries, simpler debugging, and lower operational burden, and is often a better starting point than premature service splitting. Senior engineers focus on failure modes, tradeoffs, and asking what problem an architectural choice actually solves rather than chasing impressive diagrams. The piece also warns that AI-generated architecture suggestions tend toward unnecessary completeness, and that the engineer&#39;s job is to decide what is actually needed today. It closes with a promoted paid product for practicing system design interviews.</description><link>https://daily.dev/posts/microservices-are-not-a-sign-you-re-a-senior-engineer-mdhayra8h</link><guid isPermaLink="false">https://api.daily.dev/r/mdHayRa8h</guid><pubDate>Sun, 30 Aug 2026 13:17:53 GMT</pubDate><itunes:image href="https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto/v1/posts/7cc99c8e6cd480acc14c1304004cfa46?_a=AQAEuop"></itunes:image><category>career</category><category>architecture</category><category>microservices</category><category>distributed-systems</category><category>monolith</category></item><item><title></title><description>&lt;img src=&quot;https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/s--ZrL_HSsR--/f_auto/v1722860399/public/Placeholder%2006&quot; referrerpolicy=&quot;no-referrer&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;</description><link>https://daily.dev/posts/lz5xdmgdk</link><guid isPermaLink="false">https://api.daily.dev/r/LZ5xDMgdk</guid><pubDate>Sun, 30 Aug 2026 11:32:05 GMT</pubDate><author>Damien seguy</author><itunes:image href="https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/s--ZrL_HSsR--/f_auto/v1722860399/public/Placeholder%2006"></itunes:image><category>testing</category><category>php</category><category>symfony</category></item><item><title></title><description>&lt;img src=&quot;https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/s--HRgLpUt6--/f_auto/v1722860399/public/Placeholder%2003&quot; referrerpolicy=&quot;no-referrer&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;</description><link>https://daily.dev/posts/6xq0a75yd</link><guid isPermaLink="false">https://api.daily.dev/r/6xQ0a75YD</guid><pubDate>Sun, 30 Aug 2026 07:58:38 GMT</pubDate><author>Friedrich WT</author><itunes:image href="https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/s--HRgLpUt6--/f_auto/v1722860399/public/Placeholder%2003"></itunes:image><category>javascript</category><category>performance</category><category>typescript</category><category>zod</category></item><item><title>AI makes code cheap to create, not cheap to own</title><description>&lt;img src=&quot;https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto/v1/posts/96894efcb0363eaab742228eb6ffb60a?_a=AQAEuop&quot; referrerpolicy=&quot;no-referrer&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;AI coding agents have drastically lowered the cost of generating new code, but the cost of maintaining, testing, patching, and securing that code hasn&#39;t dropped at all. An example of six applications each getting a separately AI-generated email validation implementation illustrates how duplicated code leads to inconsistent bugs across a system. Shared libraries remain valuable because a fix applied once propagates everywhere, but the piece also warns against over-applying DRY principles to every trivial snippet. The core message: don&#39;t confuse cheap-to-generate code with cheap-to-own code.</description><link>https://daily.dev/posts/ai-makes-code-cheap-to-create-not-cheap-to-own-v08wjhuwg</link><guid isPermaLink="false">https://api.daily.dev/r/V08wjhuWG</guid><pubDate>Sat, 29 Aug 2026 08:39:13 GMT</pubDate><itunes:image href="https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/f_auto,q_auto/v1/posts/96894efcb0363eaab742228eb6ffb60a?_a=AQAEuop"></itunes:image><category>architecture</category><category>ai-agents</category><category>technical-debt</category></item><item><title></title><description>&lt;img src=&quot;https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/s--1KxV4ohY--/f_auto/v1722860400/public/Placeholder%2007&quot; referrerpolicy=&quot;no-referrer&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;</description><link>https://daily.dev/posts/osxh3njb1</link><guid isPermaLink="false">https://api.daily.dev/r/oSxH3nJB1</guid><pubDate>Fri, 28 Aug 2026 14:11:18 GMT</pubDate><author>Leonid Bugaev</author><itunes:image href="https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/s--1KxV4ohY--/f_auto/v1722860400/public/Placeholder%2007"></itunes:image><category>ai-coding</category></item><item><title></title><description>&lt;img src=&quot;https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/s--CxzD6vbw--/f_auto/v1722860399/public/Placeholder%2005&quot; referrerpolicy=&quot;no-referrer&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;</description><link>https://daily.dev/posts/epjqbpcic</link><guid isPermaLink="false">https://api.daily.dev/r/epJQbPCiC</guid><pubDate>Thu, 27 Aug 2026 22:07:40 GMT</pubDate><author>Ma